hi there this is Steve here again I just  want to follow up on my last video where  I said that language learning is a is a  subconscious process and you know if  that is the case then we have to let the  language come to us and I think far too  often and language learning either the  teacher wants to force us to learn  something or we try to force ourselves  to learn something and of course it  doesn't happen that way and I want to  use a couple of recent examples I had my  discussion this morning with my one of  my Romanian tutors and of course I look  forward to these discussions because I  spent so much time listening and reading  that when I get a live native speaker I  want to speak I want to try to use what  I've learned and with my two other  tutors we have very pleasant discussions  but with this fellow I have to keep on  trying to keep them on track to do it my  way  so we start speaking and I say three  words no stop and in English no this is  not correct you must do this that and I  say never mind I just want to talk now  the system at Lync is that you can write  all my mistakes phrases in a report  which you'll send to me and I will study  them but we just want to talk okay so we  continue talking one more sentence he  stops me again it's pronounced wrong  it's this is wrong it should be that  each other this one like three or four  times and finally I got mad I said you  know is it so difficult for you to  understand I don't want you to correct  me I won't remember your Corrections and  all it does is make me lose interest in  the conversation I forget what I was  trying to say we were talking about  actually we were had a very interesting  discussion we eventually ended up having  a really interesting discussion about  the communist regime that came into  Romania after the Second World War and  the events that led up to that and what  happened in between the wars and the  various right-wing factions in Romania  and Yalta and  the West basically betrayed Eastern  Europe and we had a fascinating  discussion and I'm I know I made lots of  mistakes but I wanted to speak Romanian  oh this is part of my gradual exposure  he cannot force me to speak correctly  and all of his corrections are not going  to make me speak correctly they're just  gonna diminish my enjoyment in speaking  and they're gonna make me speak less and  they're gonna make me more inhibited why  is he trying to force me to speak  correctly before I am able to speak  correctly as I say language learning is  a subconscious process I will speak  incorrectly for a long long time and  gradually I will learn to speak more  correctly because I will notice myself a  lot of the mistakes that I make and of  course if I get his reports I'll see you  there some of the mistakes that I made  and often were conscious when we're  speaking of the things that we kind of  have trouble saying or that we're not  sure about is this right or wrong and I  think the real advantage of speaking is  that we become more aware of these areas  that you want to focus in on so that the  next time we read or listen to something  we're kind of again trying to be  attentive at least I am zooming in on  these areas with the knowledge that with  more and more exposure gradually these  things become more natural to me and  I've noticed just in the two weeks that  I've been speaking and of course  continuing my by listening and reading  I'm just able to say so much more now  and I'm able to understand so much more  so I'm conscious of the progress that I  am making but it is very much  a subconscious process so that was one  thing  another exists so there's an example of  where the teacher is trying to force you  to be better than you can be you're not  gonna be that good you're gonna make  mistakes eventually you'll get better in  my belief another person said to me you  know how do you learn idioms in a  language and I know that that a lot of  people are very intent on learning  idioms and slang so I thought about this  and I said you know what actually I paid  no attention to it  when I'm learning a language because  when I start into a language everything  is new to me the words are new the  pronunciation is new the word order the  way they say things like in Romanian  they have some of the strangest ways of  expressing the future what  quite unlike I shouldn't say strange I  you know objectively strange strange to  me quite unlike other Romance languages  so all of these things I have to get  used to it's a great big jigsaw puzzle  with only a few pieces there in the case  of Romanian more pieces than I had in  Russian or Czech because I bring with me  all the vocabulary that I have from  other Romance languages but still there  are a lot of new pieces in this new  language that I have to get used to so  every soft my screen goes blank and I  don't know what's going on anyway so  idioms so I say to myself you know I  don't worry about idioms because there's  so many other things that I have to  learn first the idioms are there you  know if there's an idiom that the Google  Translate doesn't translate for me and  I'm left with these three or four words  in a phrase that doesn't make any sense  to me I would just leave it that's not  the only thing I don't understand in the  language all of these things will  gradually subconsciously start to become  clear as I said in one of my other  videos the fog will slowly lift but you  can't force it so now if you absolutely  want to learn idioms or slang you know  fine but I don't see any necessity to do  that and I don't think it creates a very  good impression with a native speaker  because it's almost it's not expected  that the non-native speaker used these  idioms and slang and if I think you can  only use those when you are actually  very advanced in the language and where  you actually use it naturally because  again these idioms and slang or whatever  have been absorbed in some kind of a  subconscious process through lots of  reading and listening and so then you  start to use it naturally but if you try  to use it  almost artificially because you studied  it from a list of you know idiomatic  expressions it actually sounds strange  it's presumptuous because it implies a  degree of familiarity with the language  and the culture which in fact you don't  have so here again I think people who  are intent on learning idioms it's  another example of people trying to rush  things  let the language come to you the moment  will come in your studies where you will  naturally start to use idioms and much  earlier than that you'll start to  understand them now you can of course  look at a list of idioms just as I I  don't but if you're terribly interested  look at them but don't be disappointed  if you don't remember them  I look at grammar rules from time to  time I think vaguely helps me notice  things in the language but it's it seems  amazing how quickly I forget the grammar  rules that I looked at but I kind of  think it helps me be you know more  attentive so we can do that but not with  the idea of trying to learn it not with  the idea of trying to force yourself to  remember it because that all those  conscious efforts a conscious effort of  the teacher to get you to say it  correctly a conscious effort to read the  grammar rule or to study the list of  idioms all those deliberate efforts to  master the language the basic building  blocks and all of that stuff is largely  a futile effort gradually through enough  exposure through enough reading  listening using the language interacting  with people subconsciously the language  things will start to click in for you as  long as you remain motivated determined  and spend enough time with the language  so the message here is almost like a  corollary of the idea that languages are  learned basically subconsciously and  that is that we should not push things  teachers shouldn't push things at  students students shouldn't put pressure  on themselves but rather just let the  language come to you and some things  will come earlier  and fun some things will come later and  there you have it so sit back and enjoy  the journey the journey to fluency
 

